  Player hasEquipment Equipment -&gt; Incorrect
  hasEquipment domain Player -&gt; Incorrect
  Player playsIn Game -&gt; Incorrect
  playsIn domain Player -&gt; Incorrect
CQ56:
  Player hasMade InAppPurchase -&gt; Incorrect
  hasMade domain Player -&gt; Incorrect
CQ57:
  Player hasSpent Player -&gt; Incorrect
  hasSpent domain Player -&gt; Incorrect
  hasSpent range Player -&gt; Incorrect
  Player spendsAgain Player -&gt; Incorrect
  spendsAgain domain Player -&gt; Incorrect
  spendsAgain range Player -&gt; Incorrect
CQ63:
  livesIn domain Customer -&gt; Absent
CQ64:
  Player playsIn Game -&gt; Incorrect
  playsIn domain Player -&gt; Incorrect
  Player hasMade InAppPurchase -&gt; Incorrect
  hasMade domain Player -&gt; Incorrect
  Player timeSpent float -&gt; Incorrect
CQ65:
  Player hasMade InAppPurchase -&gt; Incorrect
  hasMade domain Player -&gt; Incorrect
  Player livesIn Location -&gt; Incorrect
  livesIn domain Player -&gt; Incorrect
CQ66:
  Player numberOfIaps integer -&gt; Incorrect
  Player hasMade InAppPurchase -&gt; Incorrect
  hasMade domain Player -&gt; Incorrect
  Player livesIn Location -&gt; Incorrect
  livesIn domain Player -&gt; Incorrect
Fix each failing test in the ontology: Undefined = add the term under exactly that name; Incorrect = declare it with the right type; Absent = add the tested axiom so the knowledge is modelled; Conflict = the ontology contradicts the test, correct the axioms.</prov:value>
</earl:TestResult>

